**Theorem 12.3 (at most three zeros on each complete saturated test).**
Under Assumption 10.1, every $F_{1,\epsilon,\sigma}$ has at most
three distinct circle zeros. Hence the complete endpoint inventory
in (FC.6), before any compatible-group compression, satisfies
$|Z|\le5+4\cdot3=17$.

Proof. Fix one test. Its even part is homogeneous quartic and its
odd part is $D_cL_2$, where
$L_2=(y_1-\sigma l_u-\epsilon\lambda)u+
(y_2-\sigma l_v)v$. Suppose it has four distinct zeros. By
Theorem 12.1 it is $(E_2+L_1)^2$, and all zeros lie in
$\epsilon u>0$. Neither $E_2$ nor $L_1$ can vanish identically:
otherwise the square is even under $x\mapsto-x$, which forces
forbidden antipodal zeros. Comparing odd parts on the circle gives

$$
2E_2L_1=D_cL_2
\tag{FC.40}
$$

as a homogeneous cubic identity on $\mathbb R^2$, because a
homogeneous cubic vanishing on the circle vanishes on every ray.
The positive definite binary quadratic $D_c$ is irreducible over
$\mathbb R$: a factorization into real linear factors would give
a nonzero real zero. The polynomial ring $\mathbb R[u,v]$ is a
unique factorization domain, so this irreducible is prime. It
cannot divide the nonzero linear $L_1$; (FC.40) forces
$E_2=k_cD_c$ for a nonzero real $k_c$. Divide the square root by
$k_c$ without changing its zeros. Its four zeros are those of

$$
q_c(u,v)=D_u u^2+D_v v^2+A_su+B_sv.
\tag{FC.41}
$$

Reflection in the $v$ axis allows us to put all four in $u>0$;
$D_u,D_v$ are unchanged. No zero remains on $u\le0$, since an
additional zero would give more than four zeros of the original
nonnegative degree-four test. At the two endpoints $u=0$, the
values are $D_v+B_s$ and $D_v-B_s$. They are nonzero and their
sum is $2D_v>0$. The connected opposite closed semicircle has no
zero, so $q_c$ is positive everywhere on it, including both
endpoints. If $A_s\ge0$, then
$q_c(u,v)=q_c(-u,v)+2A_su>0$ on $u>0$, a contradiction.
If $A_s<0$, parameterize that open semicircle by $v\in(-1,1)$:

$$
q_c(v)=D_u+(D_v-D_u)v^2+A_s\sqrt{1-v^2}+B_sv,\qquad
q_c''(v)=2(D_v-D_u)-\frac{A_s}{(1-v^2)^{3/2}}>0.
\tag{FC.42}
$$

A strictly convex function has at most two distinct zeros: three
zeros would contradict strict convexity at the middle one relative
to the first and third. This contradicts four. The endpoint
argument included the entire opposite arc and its endpoints;
there is no affine-chart omission. Summing the five pure contacts
and the four three-point saturated inventories proves $17$,
with coincident signed descriptions deduplicated as in Definition
3.1. This bounds candidate sheet contacts for each supplied dual,
not actual necessary labels. $\square$

